  {
    "episode_id": 96760,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96760.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 44,
    "turn_text": "Oh, you're getting old, man. Double figures. Well, I think we can do two things. One of the things that I was excited about in the Cancer Moonshot. and that Greg with 260 off-site cancer, 270, with over 7,000 people, serious docs and patient groups, et cetera, all over America, from Guam to Puerto Rico, and thousands of people assemble for the first time ever at the request of the government, and all the stakeholders are there. I hope we've done one thing. We have re-energized the notion that a lawful law is possible. making sure that everybody understands that there's so much we can do. And I hope we've come forward with 38 changes we've offered, about 20 of them on the federal government side of the ledger. For example, if we make these changes, next president's going to be in a much better position. to make some of the moves that have to be made. For example, if you go into an old diner or you go into a bar and you put money in a jukebox, you don't have to work out whether you're going to have a song played or whether you have a licensing agreement with that artist to be able to have a song played. Well, right now you have hundreds and hundreds and hundreds of drugs in trial right now developed by various companies and individuals in research. And so what happens is you have a lot of bright, young and not so young researchers saying, if we took a combination of these three drugs and combine them together, we would be able to impact exponentially on that particular cancer that's being treated. get that done now what happens is you have to reach, you can get agreement as to what you want to get this done. NIH will sign off on you being able to do that. But then you got to go work out an agreement with each of the companies relating to intellectual property and a whole range of other things. We put a new thing in place that is going to, I think, have a real impact. NIH is negotiating with, and so far we have nine or 10 biopharmaceutical firms signed up, an awful lot of the biggest philanthropies, an awful lot of the hospitals, where there's now hundreds of drugs in trial. And what you all you have to do is they've already worked out the mechanism that how you would share, how they would share and protect their investment in each of the drugs that they produced. So you don't have to work out a licensing agreement with them. And this will go from taking a year to a year and a half to be able to get permission to move with dual therapies to be able to do it in three months. And that's going to affect a lot of people's lives somewhere. I can't predict where or how or who, but it's going to increase the sense of urgency of moving forward. There's a whole range of things we can do where we can move much more rapidly. at the federal level we can move much more rapidly, where we get CMS more engaged in carrying the cost of some things, where we can get NIH more responsive directly so that they're going, taking chances earlier on with younger folks who are in, right now you want to get a grant. You have to be in a lab of somebody very established and be there for a long time in order to qualify, etc. There's ways we can speed up without in any way a negative impact on safety or security or proprietary rights that can just move this whole train down the track faster. And I think even people who don't like how hard I'm pushing on some of these things, you ask them, I think they'll tell you privately, yeah, we can move a whole lot faster. We can move a whole lot faster. Some don't like moving faster because it's not gonna be them that did it. It's not gonna be that individual who did it because there's not, shared science is not, if you're an astrophysicist, And you want a grant from DARPA or from the space agency. And you get that grant and you decide on you find some breakthrough on whether it's Saturn rings or water on Mars, whatever. You got to make that information immediately, immediately available on the web to everybody who wants it so they can all benefit from it. You do that in What was the one that was done, the cancer breakthrough, and instead of Science Magazine doing it, when the genome was finally cracked, the decision was where to publish all this information. And Science Magazine, which was more prestigious, didn't want it unless they had a proprietary interest in who could have access to it. So it was published in Nature Magazine. I'm serious. Now, as my brother would say, go figure. Imagine keeping that information under wraps for another year or year and a half because some proprietary interest. In the field of cancer, that year makes a difference between someone living and dying, whether they live another three months or six days or whatever. And so all I'm trying to do is within the range of reason, speed up the process in a way that in no way compromises public health, safety or security, does not go in and attempt to deny the benefits it should flow from the research and the development done by an individual or a company, but has some rational basis to it. You're all for kind. Thank you all so very much.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The Cancer Moonshot initiative brought together over 7,000 participants including serious doctors and patient groups from across America, including Guam and Puerto Rico.",
        "confidence": 0.98
      },
      {
        "text": "The Cancer Moonshot initiative successfully re-energized the idea that coordinated national action on cancer is possible.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Thirty-eight policy changes were proposed during the Cancer Moonshot effort, approximately twenty of which are focused on federal government actions.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "A new framework has been implemented through NIH negotiations with nine or ten biopharmaceutical firms and major philanthropies to streamline drug combination trials.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Under the new framework, researchers no longer need to negotiate individual licensing agreements with multiple companies to combine drugs in clinical trials.",
        "confidence": 0.92
